## 5. Lifecycle Integration

### Knowledge States

Both tools enter as CANDIDATE:
  - evidence_count: 2 (README analysis + source code inspection)
  - promotion requires: evidence_count >= 2 AND human approval (per [TR-07])

### Quality Dimensions (8 dimensions, applied to both tools)

  | Dimension | Heretic | Watermarks-Remover |
  |---|---|---|
  | duplicate | N/A | N/A |
  | fragmented | YES — docs split across README, config files, 9 source modules | YES — skill + service + tests + hooks |
  | orphan | No | No |
  | unused | No | No |
  | contradicted | YES — conflicts with SDD AI safety policy [SEC-01] | NO — aligns with privacy goals |
  | superseded | No | No |
  | terminology_drift | N/A | N/A |
  | architecture_drift | No | No |

### Lifecycle Paths

  Heretic: CANDIDATE → ARCHIVED (reference only, not executable)
    Rationale: Purpose contradicts security policy; knowledge retained for
    security awareness but execution is permanently BLOCKED.

  Watermarks-Remover: CANDIDATE → ACTIVE (if promoted via security review)
    Rationale: Purpose aligns with privacy goals; Layer A may be authorized
    for specific hygiene tasks with human approval.

## 6. Tool-Governance Alignment

### Heretic triggers TVL-01 through TVL-08

  TVL-01: Tool origin verification
     → p-e-w/heretic, GitHub, AGPLv3 — VERIFIED (but LEVEL 6 trust only)

  TVL-02: Capability mapping
     → All capabilities either BLOCKED or undefined (model.modify not defined)
     → Code path: main.py:177 → model.py:461 (abliterate) requires model.modify

  TVL-03: Risk assessment
     → CRITICAL (model weight modification + censorship removal)
     → Code path: model.py:461-619 (directional ablation via LoRA adapters)

  TVL-04: Permission requirements
     → BLOCKED (network.external, secret.access, system.modify all blocked)
     → Heretic imports huggingface_hub (main.py:50), uses HfApi (reproduce.py:41)

  TVL-05: Environment policy
     → DENY for all environments (no policy row even needed)

  TVL-06: Lock protocol
     → LOCKED — prohibited tool, all locks BLOCK
     → [TVL-06] When authorization cannot be resolved, execution MUST fail closed

  TVL-07: Action journal
     → @action.A45: analyze-heretic-security (ALLOW read, DENY execute)

  TVL-08: Security event
     → @security_event.S93 (heretic execution blocked)

### Watermarks-Remover triggers TVL-01 through TVL-08

  TVL-01: Tool origin verification
     → guillaumemeyer/watermarks-remover, GitHub, MIT — VERIFIED (LEVEL 6)

  TVL-02: Capability mapping
     → Layer A: code.read + code.write (EXISTS, ALLOWED)
     → Layer B: network.external (BLOCKED)

  TVL-03: Risk assessment
     → LOW for Layer A (deterministic, local)
     → HIGH for external backends (CtrlRegen, MarkLLM, MarkDiffusion)

  TVL-04: Permission requirements
     → Layer A: ALLOW (code.write on user files)
     → Layer B: BLOCKED (network.external, system.modify)

  TVL-05: Environment policy
     → Layer A: AUTO (local file cleaning)
     → Layer B: BLOCK (remote backends)

  TVL-06: Lock protocol
     → Partial lock (external backends blocked, Layer A permitted)

  TVL-07: Action journal
     → @action.A46: analyze-watermarks-remover (ALLOW read)

  TVL-08: Security event
     → @security_event.S94 (if Layer B execution attempted)

### Proposed TVL-09 (new rule for contrasted analysis)

  [TVL-09] External tools are evaluated per-capability, not as monolithic units.
  Tools whose purpose conflicts with security policy are LOCKED at the tool level
  (e.g., heretic — censorship removal conflicts with AI safety).
  Tools that align with security objectives may be partially authorized for
  specific safe sub-capabilities (e.g., watermarks-remover Layer A — privacy
  alignment permits code.read/code.write for file cleaning).

